"<h1 id=\"the-current-state-of-drs\">The Current State of DRS</h1>\n<h2 id=\"the-tldr\">The TL;DR</h2>\n<p>DRS, as it currently stands, is not worth it. The initial\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand CoP estimates demonstrate almost no uplift for what is likely\nsignificant strain on resources and an increase in car mass.</p>\n<p>In the attempt to see if DRS could be useful, I swept a combination\nof potential open-state aero configurations to see how many points we\ncould get with an optimistic setup. While these setups provide a\nbenefit, most of the point uplift comes from the acceleration event,\nwhere an “always open” configuration proves almost as effective as an\nactuated DRS setup.</p>\n<p>The value of an actuated two-state aero system is surprisingly\nminimal overall. The value of a static low-drag configuration is very\nunderrated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"current-evidence\">Current Evidence</h2>\n<h3 id=\"sensitivities\">Sensitivities</h3>\n<p>Disclaimer: <em>“All models are wrong, but some are useful.”</em>\n(George E. P. Box, 1979). The models are still in development, but I am\nfairly confident in their findings.</p>\n<figure>\n<a href=\"/draft/drs/figures/lapsim-points-tradeoff-interface.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><img src=\"/draft/drs/figures/lapsim-points-tradeoff-interface.png\"\nalt=\"Figure 1. The LapSim points tradeoff interface. Figure ID: FIG-DRS-001. Subject to change with updates.\" /></a>\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Figure 1. The LapSim points tradeoff\ninterface. Figure ID: FIG-DRS-001. Subject to change with\nupdates.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h4 id=\"point-sensitivities\">Point Sensitivities</h4>\n<p>Here are the important values that aero should keep top of mind.\nThese are subject to change by small amounts.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<caption>Table 1. Phase 4-OM local point sensitivities.</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Phase 4-OM point sensitivity</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Mass</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−0.92 scenario points per +1 kg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CG height</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−1.43 scenario points per +10 mm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Downforce coefficient</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+2.07 scenario points per +0.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Drag coefficient</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−1.53 scenario points per +0.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Frontal area</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+0.24 scenario points per +0.01 m²</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>“Phase 4-OM” is a model based on Optimum Mindstorm by Jon Vogel.\nI have verified these results with two other (less detailed) LapSim\nmodels I’ve built myself.</em></p>\n<h4 id=\"example-aero-configurations\">Example Aero Configurations</h4>\n<p>With some napkin math, these sensitivities create a table like\nthis:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<caption>Table 2. Linearized point estimates for example aero\npackages.</caption>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 9%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 12%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aero configuration</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Mass</th>\n<th\nstyle=\"text-align: right;\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D</annotation></semantics></math></th>\n<th\nstyle=\"text-align: right;\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L</annotation></semantics></math></th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Mass effect</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Drag effect</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Downforce effect</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Net scenario pts</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-optimized wings</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">10 kg</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1.20</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1.70</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−9.22 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−18.36 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+35.17 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>+7.59 pts</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Optimized wings</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">10 kg</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1.10</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">2.20</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−9.22 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−16.83 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+45.52 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>+19.47 pts</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Optimized wings + undertray</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">17 kg</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1.40</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">2.80</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−15.67 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−21.42 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+57.93 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>+20.84 pts</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-optimized wings + undertray</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">17 kg</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1.50</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1.90</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−15.67 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−22.95 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+39.31 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>+0.69 pts</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>This table does not capture aero balance or nonlinear effects. That\nis where the LapSim grids become useful. I give these because they are\nextremely valuable for napkin math.</p>\n<p>Notably, aero efficiency,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L/C_D</annotation></semantics></math>,\nis not the target metric anymore. This could suggest pushing a\nlightweight aero package to higher\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L</annotation></semantics></math>\nvalues, as its sensitivity outpaces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D</annotation></semantics></math>.\nI hope that the plots make this more obvious.</p>\n<h3 id=\"initial-drs-lap-time-results\">Initial DRS Lap-Time Results</h3>\n<p>This simulation doesn’t include the mass added by DRS. My intuition\nsays the supplied DRS values could be better, but that is purely\nintuition. The results are shockingly small. This case also moves the\nopen-state CoP dramatically forward, so it is not the cleaner fixed-CoP\nsweep discussed later.</p>\n<h4 id=\"initial-configuration\">Initial Configuration</h4>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<caption>Table 3. Initial supplied closed and open DRS\nconfigurations.</caption>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 15%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 21%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 21%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 21%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 21%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>DRS state</th>\n<th\nstyle=\"text-align: right;\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D</annotation></semantics></math></th>\n<th\nstyle=\"text-align: right;\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L</annotation></semantics></math>\nmagnitude</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">CoP behind front axle</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Front aero balance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Closed</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1.05</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1.74</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.69 m</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">55.5% front</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Open</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.50</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.50</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.02 m</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">98.7% front</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Change, open − closed</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−0.55</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−1.24</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−0.67 m</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+43.2%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Reference area is 1.1 m². Positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L</annotation></semantics></math>\nis downforce and positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D</annotation></semantics></math>\nis drag. In my opinion, 98% front balance is extreme, but it’s the spec\nI was given. Further sweeps use the more balanced 55% (0.69 m behind the\nfront axle) for both open and closed configs, as it removes the\noff-balance aero variable.</p>\n<h4 id=\"lap-time-deltas\">Lap-Time Deltas</h4>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<caption>Table 4. Lap-time change relative to the always-closed\nconfiguration.</caption>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 15%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 21%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 21%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 21%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 21%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Solver</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Autocross open Δ</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Autocross actuated Δ</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Endurance open Δ</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Endurance actuated Δ</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Phase 3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+0.908</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>−0.013</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+1.436</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>−0.018</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phase 4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+0.935</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>−0.012</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+1.500</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+0.005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phase 4-OM</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+0.840</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>−0.018</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+1.298</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>−0.015</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Here,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t = t_{\\mathrm{state}} - t_{\\mathrm{closed}}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nNegative is faster. The Autocross baseline is about 45 seconds and\nEndurance is about 75 seconds.</p>\n<h4 id=\"actuation-assumptions\">Actuation Assumptions</h4>\n<p>The idealized model switches instantly wherever DRS is optimal. It is\nopen for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autocross: <strong>6.2% of distance</strong></li>\n<li>Endurance: <strong>4.6% of distance</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is largely driven by how effective the DRS config is. If the\nvalues are more favorable, it will spend more time in its actuated open\nstate.</p>\n<h3 id=\"skidpad-and-acceleration\">Skidpad and Acceleration</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<caption>Table 5. Skidpad and Acceleration results for the initial\nconfiguration.</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Event/model</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Closed</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Actuated</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Skidpad (Phase 4-OM)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">5.209</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">5.209 <strong>(0.0%)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Acceleration (Dedicated Model)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">4.506</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">4.446 <strong>(86.1%)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Skidpad remains closed because drag reduction does not prove as\nimportant as the additional downforce. The Acceleration event shows a\nlarge benefit from the low-drag state.</p>\n<h3 id=\"initial-scenario-point-impact\">Initial Scenario-Point\nImpact</h3>\n<p>These use the earlier synthetic scoring contract: a notional winner\n5% faster than each solver’s closed baseline. Efficiency is excluded.\nPositive is better.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<caption>Table 6. Initial synthetic scenario-point impact relative to\nalways closed.</caption>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 11%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 11%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 15%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 15%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 15%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 15%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 15%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Solver</th>\n<th>State</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Accel Δ</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Skidpad Δ</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Autocross Δ</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Endurance Δ</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Total Δ</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Phase 4-OM</td>\n<td>Always closed</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.00</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.00</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.00</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.00</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>0.00</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phase 4-OM</td>\n<td>Always open</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+3.82</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−5.83</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−6.26</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−13.22</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>−21.48</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phase 4-OM</td>\n<td>Actuated</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+3.67</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">0.00</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+0.13</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+0.16</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>+3.96</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Almost the entire actuated-DRS benefit came from the acceleration\nevent. The always-open acceleration setup was slightly better than the\nactuated one due to some of the actuator model controller gates, as I\nran these sweeps using the gated actuation model.</p>\n<p>In the past, we sometimes manually set an always-open rear\nconfiguration for Acceleration, and this supports the idea that it\nhelped compared with the full-downforce setup.</p>\n<h2 id=\"checkpoint-2-giving-drs-its-best-shot\">Checkpoint 2: Giving DRS\nIts Best Shot</h2>\n<p>The next step was to give DRS its best shot. I swept open-state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L</annotation></semantics></math>\nover two closed baselines:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lower-aero baseline: closed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D = 1.05</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1.74</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|C_L| = 1.74</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n<li>Higher-aero baseline: closed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D = 1.60</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3.20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|C_L| = 3.20</annotation></semantics></math></li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model boundaries.</strong> The later sweeps fix CoP at 0.69 m\nbehind the front axle (55% front balance) and use idealized, zero-delay\nfixed-zone actuation. DRS mass is excluded. Skidpad is assumed closed\nrather than simulated. Scenario points use the synthetic scoring\ncontract described above.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"where-acceleration-actually-benefits-from-actuation\">Where\nAcceleration Actually Benefits From Actuation</h3>\n<figure>\n<a href=\"/draft/drs/figures/drs-lower-aero-acceleration-phase-comparison.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><img\nsrc=\"/draft/drs/figures/drs-lower-aero-acceleration-phase-comparison.png\"\nalt=\"Figure 2a. Lower-aero Acceleration sweep. Left: best idealized policy. Right: total point gain versus always closed. Figure ID: FIG-DRS-002, lower-aero panel.\" /></a>\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Figure 2a. Lower-aero Acceleration sweep.\nLeft: best idealized policy. Right: total point gain versus always\nclosed. Figure ID: FIG-DRS-002, lower-aero panel.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<figure>\n<a href=\"/draft/drs/figures/drs-higher-aero-acceleration-phase-comparison.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><img\nsrc=\"/draft/drs/figures/drs-higher-aero-acceleration-phase-comparison.png\"\nalt=\"Figure 2b. Higher-aero Acceleration sweep. Left: best idealized policy. Right: total point gain versus always closed. Figure ID: FIG-DRS-002, higher-aero panel.\" /></a>\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Figure 2b. Higher-aero Acceleration\nsweep. Left: best idealized policy. Right: total point gain versus\nalways closed. Figure ID: FIG-DRS-002, higher-aero panel.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p>All three models give the same policy boundary. Most of the\nacceleration benefit comes from the low-drag open state itself.\nActuation only helps where starting closed improves the launch.</p>\n<h3 id=\"points-available-outside-acceleration\">Points Available Outside\nAcceleration</h3>\n<figure>\n<a href=\"/draft/drs/figures/drs-higher-aero-non-acceleration-phase-comparison.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><img\nsrc=\"/draft/drs/figures/drs-higher-aero-non-acceleration-phase-comparison.png\"\nalt=\"Figure 3a. Higher-aero fixed-zone DRS gains outside Acceleration. Endurance and Autocross are compared with DRS always closed; Skidpad is assumed closed. Figure ID: FIG-DRS-003, higher-aero panel.\" /></a>\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Figure 3a. Higher-aero fixed-zone DRS\ngains outside Acceleration. Endurance and Autocross are compared with\nDRS always closed; Skidpad is assumed closed. Figure ID: FIG-DRS-003,\nhigher-aero panel.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<figure>\n<a href=\"/draft/drs/figures/drs-lower-aero-non-acceleration-phase-comparison.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><img\nsrc=\"/draft/drs/figures/drs-lower-aero-non-acceleration-phase-comparison.png\"\nalt=\"Figure 3b. Lower-aero fixed-zone DRS gains outside Acceleration. Endurance and Autocross are compared with DRS always closed; Skidpad is assumed closed. Figure ID: FIG-DRS-003, lower-aero panel.\" /></a>\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Figure 3b. Lower-aero fixed-zone DRS\ngains outside Acceleration. Endurance and Autocross are compared with\nDRS always closed; Skidpad is assumed closed. Figure ID: FIG-DRS-003,\nlower-aero panel.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p>Endurance and Autocross follow the obvious direction: reduce drag\nwhile keeping as much downforce as possible. Even with a very good\npackage, the combined upper bound for Endurance, Autocross, and Skidpad\nis modest:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n<caption>Table 7. Combined non-Acceleration upper bound by LapSim\nmodel.</caption>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 20%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 26%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 26%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 26%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Closed baseline</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Phase 3</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Phase 4</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Phase 4-OM</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Lower aero:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D = 1.05</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1.74</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|C_L| = 1.74</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+1.10 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+0.83 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+0.90 pts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Higher aero:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D = 1.60</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3.20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|C_L| = 3.20</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+1.72 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+1.53 pts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+1.58 pts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>That is less than two points for an idealized, high-performing\npackage and closer to one point for the lower-aero case. This was\nhonestly very surprising.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-could-change-the-answer\">What Could Change the Answer?</h2>\n<p>My models are not perfect, but I am reasonably confident in the\ndirection because the sweep includes optimistic, well-optimized DRS\nstates. The magnitude still depends on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The aero estimates\n<ul>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand CoP.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>The actuation\n<ul>\n<li>instant full actuation (unrealistic)</li>\n<li>driver or controller limitations</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>The DRS mass add is not included.</li>\n<li>The scenario-point scoring assumptions</li>\n<li>The result is based on digitized tracks and may change with track\ncharacter\n<ul>\n<li>Faster tracks benefit much more from aerodynamics</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>The LapSim could still have errors\n<ul>\n<li>Audit in progress</li>\n<li>I occasionally still find small bugs</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The track question is worth its own sweep. Past sensitivities showed\nlittle aero benefit on slow, sharp tracks and more on smooth, high-speed\ntracks, matching the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^2</annotation></semantics></math>\nnature of aerodynamic force. A useful next check is to make the track\nsharper or smoother and see how much the conclusion moves. I’ve done\nthese in the past for the EV, which is much more sensitive to energy use\nthan the IC car.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-static-aero-package\">The Static Aero Package</h3>\n<figure>\n<a href=\"/draft/drs/figures/static-aero-package-point-surface.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\"><img src=\"/draft/drs/figures/static-aero-package-point-surface.png\"\nalt=\"Figure 4. Static aero-package point surface. Isolines show configurations with equal modeled scenario-point value.\" /></a>\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Figure 4. Static aero-package point\nsurface. Isolines show configurations with equal modeled scenario-point\nvalue.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p>In my opinion,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L = 3.2</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a realistic upper bound for a front wing + rear wing + undertray car\ndeveloped by our team. Doing this with a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D</annotation></semantics></math>\nof 0.5 is quite unrealistic, but that would be the perfect config.</p>\n<p>I’d like to draw attention to the isolines. A static low-drag package\nwith a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D</annotation></semantics></math>\nof 0.5 needs a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L</annotation></semantics></math>\nof 1.8 (aero efficiency of 3.6) to match the performance of a package\nwith a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D</annotation></semantics></math>\nof 1.6 and a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L</annotation></semantics></math>\nof 2.8 (aero efficiency of 1.75).</p>\n<p>A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L/C_D</annotation></semantics></math>\nratio of 3.6 may be impossible to achieve, but 1.75 may be very\nrealistic. In the future, I will also see if I can incorporate the\nEfficiency event or gauge how much energy is used in braking compared to\naero losses. My intuition tells me it’s low, which is why I generally\ngive the guidance, “go for a higher absolute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L</annotation></semantics></math>,\neven if aero efficiency is lower.”</p>\n<h2 id=\"conclusion\">Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Based on the current estimates, I don’t think DRS is worth\nprioritizing on this car. That is honestly neither the outcome I\nexpected nor the one I wanted. I am not declaring DRS useless, but I\ndon’t think it’s worth the development time unless better aero estimates\nshow a larger benefit. Right now, a dedicated low-drag Acceleration\nsetup appears to capture most of the benefit without requiring a full\nDRS system. It’s possible that resources are best spent creating the\nbest static aero package, and maybe some investigation is done into\ncreating an optimized open config for the acceleration event.</p>\n<h2 id=\"guiding-questions\">Guiding Questions</h2>\n<h3 id=\"what-problem-am-i-actually-trying-to-solve\">What problem am I\nactually trying to solve?</h3>\n<p>Maximize performance under limited resources by justifying good\nprojects and cutting low-ROI ones. We have limited resources.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-would-it-take-to-solve-it\">What would it take to solve\nit?</h3>\n<p>Good\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand CoP estimates from a full-car simulation we trust.</p>\n<p>A reasonably reliable LapSim model that captures the effects of\naero.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-does-that-outcome-mean-in-specific-observable-terms\">What\ndoes that outcome mean in specific, observable terms?</h3>\n<p>The aero team describes projects in terms of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_D</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_L</annotation></semantics></math>,\nCoP, weight, cost, time, and certainty. I can then estimate the points\nuplift and compare it with the resources required.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-should-we-do-next\">What should we do next?</h3>\n<p>Continue low-cost development long enough to improve the estimates,\nthen decide whether DRS is worth the cost and weight. Even if it is not\nworthwhile now, this could be useful groundwork for evaluating DRS on\nthe next EV.</p>\n"